804
00:35:14,557 --> 00:35:17,073
"Algorithms of Oppression," 2018,

805
00:35:18,060 --> 00:35:20,160
which exposed how search engines

806
00:35:20,160 --> 00:35:23,910
systematically misrepresented women of color

807
00:35:23,910 --> 00:35:27,060
and perpetuated systemic inequalities.

808
00:35:27,060 --> 00:35:29,370
Her work has become even more urgent

809
00:35:29,370 --> 00:35:31,710
as we grapple with large language models

810
00:35:31,710 --> 00:35:33,390
trained on public internet data

811
00:35:33,390 --> 00:35:37,053
that can amplify and scale these very biases.

812
00:35:38,400 --> 00:35:42,090
She'll be in conversation with author Karen Hao,

813
00:35:42,090 --> 00:35:44,880
one of the foremost tech journalists,

814
00:35:44,880 --> 00:35:47,790
covering AI with pieces in The Atlantic,

815
00:35:47,790 --> 00:35:50,880
a leader of the Pulitzer AI series,

816
00:35:50,880 --> 00:35:55,230
and whose recent and influential book "Empires of AI:

817
00:35:55,230 --> 00:35:59,490
The Dreams and Nightmares in Sam Altman's OpenAI,"

818
00:35:59,490 --> 00:36:01,770
provides a comprehensive examination

819
00:36:01,770 --> 00:36:05,310
of how artificial intelligence shapes power structures,

820
00:36:05,310 --> 00:36:08,943
reinforces existing inequalities and impacts social justice.

821
00:36:10,620 --> 00:36:14,790
Hao's analysis extends to AI's devastating climate impact,

822
00:36:14,790 --> 00:36:18,630
revealing how the technology's massive energy demands

823
00:36:18,630 --> 00:36:20,550
and resource extraction

824
00:36:20,550 --> 00:36:23,133
further entrench global inequalities.

825
00:36:24,540 --> 00:36:28,200
Now, as community college leaders

826
00:36:28,200 --> 00:36:30,360
serving students who have historically

827
00:36:30,360 --> 00:36:32,430
been underrepresented in higher education

828
00:36:32,430 --> 00:36:36,930
and often marginalized by technological systems,

829
00:36:36,930 --> 00:36:38,340
we have a responsibility

830
00:36:38,340 --> 00:36:40,623
to embrace technological innovation.

831
00:36:41,490 --> 00:36:46,490
Even as we interrogate the use and impact of these tools,

832
00:36:46,500 --> 00:36:50,580
it's our responsibility to provide the type of education

833
00:36:50,580 --> 00:36:53,130
and skill sets that will allow our learners

834
00:36:53,130 --> 00:36:55,863
to also interrogate those tools.

835
00:36:56,970 --> 00:37:01,560
This conversation between these two influential thinkers

836
00:37:01,560 --> 00:37:04,230
offers us critical perspectives on ensuring

837
00:37:04,230 --> 00:37:08,070
our technological choices advance equity

838
00:37:08,070 --> 00:37:10,560
rather than perpetuate harm.

839
00:37:10,560 --> 00:37:11,940
Their leadership reminds us

840
00:37:11,940 --> 00:37:14,670
that the voices guiding our digital future

841
00:37:14,670 --> 00:37:17,673
must reflect the diversity of the communities we serve.

842
00:37:18,570 --> 00:37:21,150
So with that, please give an enthusiastic welcome

843
00:37:21,150 --> 00:37:24,990
to my dear friend Dr. Safiya Noble and Karen Hao

844
00:37:24,990 --> 00:37:27,570
for what will undoubtedly be an illuminating

845
00:37:27,570 --> 00:37:29,282
and necessary dialogue.

