1321
01:00:18,840 --> 01:00:21,270
that are coming out of large language models

1322
01:00:21,270 --> 01:00:26,270
that are undermining our profitability that we're not sold."

1323
01:00:27,750 --> 01:00:29,610
And, you know, I always think

1324
01:00:29,610 --> 01:00:31,080
like this isn't the first time

1325
01:00:31,080 --> 01:00:35,520
when chatbots created for, I don't know, American Airlines,

1326
01:00:35,520 --> 01:00:38,640
and then they're like, "No, not working for us.

1327
01:00:38,640 --> 01:00:40,530
But you know what? You could go sell it to UCLA.

1328
01:00:40,530 --> 01:00:42,327
They definitely should take it."

1329
01:00:42,327 --> 01:00:43,160
You know what I'm saying?

1330
01:00:43,160 --> 01:00:45,390
Like, it's definitely good enough for them.

1331
01:00:45,390 --> 01:00:49,470
So I think about this kind of these products

1332
01:00:49,470 --> 01:00:52,470
and companies in search of markets,

1333
01:00:52,470 --> 01:00:55,650
and I think that you really covered that so well.

1334
01:00:55,650 --> 01:00:59,010
- Yeah, I mean, one of the things that,

1335
01:00:59,010 --> 01:01:03,297
obviously I have a lot of beef with Silicon Valley. (laughs)

1336
01:01:03,297 --> 01:01:04,890
But one of them is, you know,

1337
01:01:04,890 --> 01:01:06,540
when I was studying engineering,

1338
01:01:06,540 --> 01:01:10,350
we always learned that you first figure out

1339
01:01:10,350 --> 01:01:12,570
the challenge and scope it,

1340
01:01:12,570 --> 01:01:15,150
and then you figure out what kind of technology

1341
01:01:15,150 --> 01:01:17,490
do you build to tackle that challenge.

1342
01:01:17,490 --> 01:01:19,500
Because at the end of the day,

1343
01:01:19,500 --> 01:01:23,160
technology is meant to improve our lives.

1344
01:01:23,160 --> 01:01:27,900
And in the last decade plus, for Silicon Valley,

1345
01:01:27,900 --> 01:01:32,900
that ethos around engineering solutions to existing problems

1346
01:01:32,970 --> 01:01:34,110
has gone out the window,

1347
01:01:34,110 --> 01:01:37,620
and they advance technology for technology's sake.

1348
01:01:37,620 --> 01:01:39,360
And then you run into an issue

1349
01:01:39,360 --> 01:01:41,820
where you don't have product market fit,

1350
01:01:41,820 --> 01:01:44,400
but they've already poured hundreds of billions,

1351
01:01:44,400 --> 01:01:47,370
trillions of dollars into producing this technology.

1352
01:01:47,370 --> 01:01:50,430
So now they have to seek that business,

1353
01:01:50,430 --> 01:01:51,480
they have to figure out

1354
01:01:51,480 --> 01:01:53,340
where they're going to get that revenue from.

1355
01:01:53,340 --> 01:01:57,060
And the information just reported last week

1356
01:01:57,060 --> 01:01:59,730
that OpenAI originally projected

1357
01:01:59,730 --> 01:02:04,380
that they would roughly have a $35 billion cash burn,

1358
01:02:04,380 --> 01:02:08,670
and they accidentally underprojected by $80 billion.

1359
01:02:08,670 --> 01:02:11,400
So they now have updated their projections

1360
01:02:11,400 --> 01:02:15,840
saying that they're gonna have a $115 billion cash burn

1361
01:02:15,840 --> 01:02:18,093
in the next three years.

1362
01:02:20,220 --> 01:02:23,460
So they are $115 billion in the hole.

1363
01:02:23,460 --> 01:02:25,890
And where do you think they're going to go find that money?

1364
01:02:25,890 --> 01:02:26,985
Anywhere.

1365
01:02:26,985 --> 01:02:29,250
Anyone that will give them money

1366
01:02:29,250 --> 01:02:32,100
they're going to seek business with.

1367
01:02:32,100 --> 01:02:33,510
And they're going to tell you,

1368
01:02:33,510 --> 01:02:36,300
I mean, the other beef I have

1369
01:02:36,300 --> 01:02:38,970
is they are ultimately selling a vision of AI

1370
01:02:38,970 --> 01:02:41,970
that I call the everything machine.

1371
01:02:41,970 --> 01:02:44,137
They're coming to everyone saying,

1372
01:02:44,137 --> 01:02:48,240
"This AI, artificial general intelligence, AGI,

1373
01:02:48,240 --> 01:02:49,950
is going to cure cancer,

1374
01:02:49,950 --> 01:02:51,780
it's gonna solve climate change,

1375
01:02:51,780 --> 01:02:53,760
it's going to eradicate poverty,

1376
01:02:53,760 --> 01:02:56,430
it's going to fix all of our educational problems."

1377
01:02:56,430 --> 01:02:58,200
And throughout history,

1378
01:02:58,200 --> 01:03:00,450
we have always seen time and time again

1379
01:03:00,450 --> 01:03:02,070
that when someone gives you a potion

1380
01:03:02,070 --> 01:03:03,450
that's going to fix all of your problems,

1381
01:03:03,450 --> 01:03:05,430
they're probably charlatans.

1382
01:03:05,430 --> 01:03:07,163
And this is what Silicon Valley is doing.

1383
01:03:08,632 --> 01:03:09,465
(audience applauding) (audience member cheers)

1384
01:03:09,465 --> 01:03:11,760
And it's because they need that solution

1385
01:03:11,760 --> 01:03:12,960
to be in everything machine

1386
01:03:12,960 --> 01:03:15,870
so that they can fill that $150 billion hole.

1387
01:03:15,870 --> 01:03:16,703
- Yeah, absolutely.

1388
01:03:16,703 --> 01:03:18,630
And I think we have to remember

1389
01:03:18,630 --> 01:03:20,987
that they're not gonna get the money out of us,

1390
01:03:20,987 --> 01:03:23,518
'cause we don't have any money.

1391
01:03:23,518 --> 01:03:25,321
(audience laughs)

1392
01:03:25,321 --> 01:03:28,230
But we have the data, and our students have the data.

1393
01:03:28,230 --> 01:03:30,270
And I think about this, you know, all the time.

1394
01:03:30,270 --> 01:03:33,510
I remember when I was, a long time ago,

1395
01:03:33,510 --> 01:03:36,930
when Turnitin became the big hot thing.

1396
01:03:36,930 --> 01:03:40,350
And I remember, my friends and I in grad school,

1397
01:03:40,350 --> 01:03:42,030
we wouldn't use Turnitin.

1398
01:03:42,030 --> 01:03:43,950
We wouldn't let any of our students use it,

1399
01:03:43,950 --> 01:03:46,500
even though the university wanted us to do that,

1400
01:03:46,500 --> 01:03:47,460
check for plagiarism.

1401
01:03:47,460 --> 01:03:49,620
We're like, "What if we taught our students

1402
01:03:49,620 --> 01:03:52,350
that cheating only hurt themselves," you know?

1403
01:03:52,350 --> 01:03:55,173
Like, what if we actually approached it that way?

1404
01:03:56,910 --> 01:03:59,400
Because, you know, the papers

1405
01:03:59,400 --> 01:04:04,110
that a student writes when they're 18 or 25 or 40

1406
01:04:04,110 --> 01:04:09,110
to their instructor is, you know, it's personal.

1407
01:04:09,780 --> 01:04:14,730
It's full of a lot of half-baked ideas sometimes,

1408
01:04:14,730 --> 01:04:18,600
it's experimental, it's ideation.

1409
01:04:18,600 --> 01:04:20,550
It's not probably a thing you want

1410
01:04:20,550 --> 01:04:23,010
in a searchable database later on

1411
01:04:23,010 --> 01:04:24,150
when they're going for a job

1412
01:04:24,150 --> 01:04:26,520
that an employer can pull up and find,

1413
01:04:26,520 --> 01:04:30,007
and say like, "Oh, what were you thinking when you were 18?

1414
01:04:30,007 --> 01:04:31,380
And now we're gonna use that against you,"

1415
01:04:31,380 --> 01:04:33,720
or, "How do we build a data profile about you

1416
01:04:33,720 --> 01:04:36,060
based on all your past research papers," right?

1417
01:04:36,060 --> 01:04:39,300
I mean, there's like a lot of practical, ethical reasons

1418
01:04:39,300 --> 01:04:43,170
why we would not wanna put students' ideas

1419
01:04:43,170 --> 01:04:45,840
or faculties' intellectual property

1420
01:04:45,840 --> 01:04:49,140
or all of, you know, staff decisions into these systems

1421
01:04:49,140 --> 01:04:52,890
only to have them used as a type of predictive analytic

1422
01:04:52,890 --> 01:04:54,540
about people for the future.

1423
01:04:54,540 --> 01:04:57,870
And I know you have a lot of thoughts about what that means.

1424
01:04:57,870 --> 01:05:01,560
I mean, our field, we write about the harms

1425
01:05:01,560 --> 01:05:03,390
of predictive analytics

1426
01:05:03,390 --> 01:05:06,363
constantly predicting the past into the future.

1427
01:05:07,380 --> 01:05:10,140
I really wanna hear your take on that.

1428
01:05:10,140 --> 01:05:12,990
- Yeah, I mean, a lot of the entire premise of AI,

1429
01:05:12,990 --> 01:05:16,950
I mean, is you are training on data that existed

1430
01:05:16,950 --> 01:05:21,000
and then using that to predict the potentials of people,

1431
01:05:21,000 --> 01:05:24,060
what they are allowed to be in the future.

1432
01:05:24,060 --> 01:05:27,870
And of course, if you're only looking at past data,

1433
01:05:27,870 --> 01:05:30,570
then you're constraining the possibilities

1434
01:05:30,570 --> 01:05:33,000
of the future to look exactly like the past,

1435
01:05:33,000 --> 01:05:34,410
if not a worse version of the past.

1436
01:05:34,410 --> 01:05:36,810
We've now seen an abundance of research

1437
01:05:36,810 --> 01:05:41,003
to show that when training AI models on historical data,

1438
01:05:41,003 --> 01:05:44,250
that it actually exacerbates the fault lines

1439
01:05:44,250 --> 01:05:46,770
in that historical data and projects it

1440
01:05:46,770 --> 01:05:49,320
and amplifies it in the future.

1441
01:05:49,320 --> 01:05:51,660
And that's sort of antithetical

1442
01:05:51,660 --> 01:05:53,740
to the entire project of education, isn't it?

1443
01:05:53,740 --> 01:05:57,030
Like, we engage in education

1444
01:05:57,030 --> 01:05:59,790
so that we can have a fundamentally different future

1445
01:05:59,790 --> 01:06:02,400
than the mistakes that we've made in the past.

1446
01:06:02,400 --> 01:06:05,053
And so, absolutely, I-

1447
01:06:05,053 --> 01:06:07,860
(audience applauding)

1448
01:06:07,860 --> 01:06:10,480
I think that

1449
01:06:14,010 --> 01:06:17,340
our willingness to believe

1450
01:06:17,340 --> 01:06:21,510
that somehow a technology that works in this way

1451
01:06:21,510 --> 01:06:25,860
will fortify that project of education is,

1452
01:06:25,860 --> 01:06:27,270
yeah, it's really concerning

1453
01:06:27,270 --> 01:06:32,270
because ultimately that is not what these tools are about,

1454
01:06:33,000 --> 01:06:35,370
especially the way that Silicon Valley designs them.

1455
01:06:35,370 --> 01:06:37,110
- Yeah, I think about,

1456
01:06:37,110 --> 01:06:39,300
you know, you said, tell my students

1457
01:06:39,300 --> 01:06:40,690
the reason why

1458
01:06:43,110 --> 01:06:45,990
3 shirts for 20 bucks at the Gap

1459
01:06:45,990 --> 01:06:48,210
feels so good, like such a great deal,

1460
01:06:48,210 --> 01:06:52,500
is because it costs $100,000 to get a degree.

1461
01:06:52,500 --> 01:06:57,500
Like, the instinct for like the wanting

1462
01:06:57,780 --> 01:07:01,110
the easy thing that looks amazing

1463
01:07:01,110 --> 01:07:03,720
without any understanding of the cost

1464
01:07:03,720 --> 01:07:06,840
that goes into 3 shirts for 20 bucks,

1465
01:07:06,840 --> 01:07:08,350
3 sweaters for 20 bucks

1466
01:07:09,720 --> 01:07:12,780
sits in the context of what we're losing,

1467
01:07:12,780 --> 01:07:15,750
of what it feels further and further out of reach

1468
01:07:15,750 --> 01:07:17,220
in our democracy.

1469
01:07:17,220 --> 01:07:22,220
And I love the part, and by loved, I mean I wept,

1470
01:07:22,230 --> 01:07:24,690
the part of your book where you talk about

1471
01:07:24,690 --> 01:07:27,450
who pays the price in the Global South.

1472
01:07:27,450 --> 01:07:29,550
And I think that, you know, for Americans,

1473
01:07:29,550 --> 01:07:31,980
sometimes it's really hard to understand

1474
01:07:31,980 --> 01:07:33,480
because they've never been to Paraguay,

1475
01:07:33,480 --> 01:07:36,510
they've never been to Chile, they don't really understand.

1476
01:07:36,510 --> 01:07:38,640
But I think it's important

1477
01:07:38,640 --> 01:07:41,637
for us to talk about the Kenyan data workers

1478
01:07:41,637 --> 01:07:45,270
and the water and environmental costs.

1479
01:07:45,270 --> 01:07:47,130
- Yeah, so part of my book,

1480
01:07:47,130 --> 01:07:49,530
I wanted to tell the inside story of OpenAI,

1481
01:07:49,530 --> 01:07:52,560
but I really did not want to just stay within the Valley.

1482
01:07:52,560 --> 01:07:55,560
Because if you tell the story of AI, or any technology,

1483
01:07:55,560 --> 01:07:58,778
by only staying in one place where it actually works,

1484
01:07:58,778 --> 01:08:00,540
you are not going to get a clear picture

1485
01:08:00,540 --> 01:08:03,420
of the actual fallout that can happen globally,

1486
01:08:03,420 --> 01:08:06,270
especially for marginalized and vulnerable populations.

1487
01:08:06,270 --> 01:08:07,890
So I traveled a lot for the book.

1488
01:08:07,890 --> 01:08:11,010
I went to Kenya, I drew upon reporting from South Africa,

1489
01:08:11,010 --> 01:08:13,560
I went to Chile, I went to Hawaii, Colombia

1490
01:08:13,560 --> 01:08:17,670
to really understand how these communities at the margins

1491
01:08:17,670 --> 01:08:21,270
are actually experiencing this technology revolution

1492
01:08:21,270 --> 01:08:23,370
that's supposed to bring this into the future.

1493
01:08:23,370 --> 01:08:25,650
And so in Kenya, I met with workers

1494
01:08:25,650 --> 01:08:28,110
who had been contracted by OpenAI

1495
01:08:28,110 --> 01:08:30,030
to support the development

1496
01:08:30,030 --> 01:08:32,640
of what ultimately became ChatGPT.

1497
01:08:32,640 --> 01:08:35,550
And what OpenAI was asking these workers to do

1498
01:08:35,550 --> 01:08:38,490
was help them build a content moderation filter

1499
01:08:38,490 --> 01:08:41,250
that would sorta staple onto the models

1500
01:08:41,250 --> 01:08:43,380
that they were developing, the large language models,

1501
01:08:43,380 --> 01:08:46,410
and ensure that when users actually talk

1502
01:08:46,410 --> 01:08:49,230
with this text generation system that can generate anything,

1503
01:08:49,230 --> 01:08:52,770
that it doesn't end up generating racist, toxic hate speech

1504
01:08:52,770 --> 01:08:55,980
because then it would not be a commercial success.

1505
01:08:55,980 --> 01:08:57,870
And so for the Kenyan workers,

1506
01:08:57,870 --> 01:09:01,650
what that meant was they had to wade through

1507
01:09:01,650 --> 01:09:06,650
reams and reams of the worst content on the internet,

1508
01:09:06,810 --> 01:09:08,760
as well as AI-generated content

1509
01:09:08,760 --> 01:09:11,670
where OpenAI was prompting its own AI models

1510
01:09:11,670 --> 01:09:15,900
to imagine the worst possible content you could find

1511
01:09:15,900 --> 01:09:17,100
on the internet.

1512
01:09:17,100 --> 01:09:20,670
And then they had to label and put these pieces of text

1513
01:09:20,670 --> 01:09:23,550
into a detailed taxonomy that OpenAI provided them.

1514
01:09:23,550 --> 01:09:26,340
Is this hate speech? Is this violent speech?

1515
01:09:26,340 --> 01:09:28,380
Is this sexual content?

1516
01:09:28,380 --> 01:09:30,810
Is this sexual content that involves abuse

1517
01:09:30,810 --> 01:09:32,370
and abuse of children?

1518
01:09:32,370 --> 01:09:36,420
So they were asking them to do these detailed evaluations

1519
01:09:36,420 --> 01:09:40,710
of how bad is this content and what kind of badness is it

1520
01:09:40,710 --> 01:09:44,280
so that when they trained this content moderation filter,

1521
01:09:44,280 --> 01:09:46,770
that filter would then be able to distinguish

1522
01:09:46,770 --> 01:09:51,660
between these things and block it out of ChatGPT.

1523
01:09:51,660 --> 01:09:53,370
These workers ended up having

1524
01:09:53,370 --> 01:09:56,040
the same exact psychological impacts

1525
01:09:56,040 --> 01:09:59,370
that content moderators of the social media era had.

1526
01:09:59,370 --> 01:10:01,860
They ended up deeply traumatized.

1527
01:10:01,860 --> 01:10:06,330
They ended up with completely altered personalities.

1528
01:10:06,330 --> 01:10:10,620
Many of them were extroverted and warm and social

1529
01:10:10,620 --> 01:10:12,060
before they started the project,

1530
01:10:12,060 --> 01:10:15,210
and then after became extremely socially anxious,

1531
01:10:15,210 --> 01:10:16,710
very isolated.

1532
01:10:16,710 --> 01:10:19,770
And I talk about this one man, Mophat Okinyi,

1533
01:10:19,770 --> 01:10:22,350
who was part of this group of Kenyan workers

1534
01:10:22,350 --> 01:10:25,830
that was on the sexual content team for OpenAI.

1535
01:10:25,830 --> 01:10:30,180
And as his personality did a complete 180,

1536
01:10:30,180 --> 01:10:33,630
he didn't know how to explain this to his wife

1537
01:10:33,630 --> 01:10:35,250
and to his stepdaughter

1538
01:10:35,250 --> 01:10:37,777
because he didn't know how to tell them,

1539
01:10:37,777 --> 01:10:42,330
"My job involves reading sex content all day."

1540
01:10:42,330 --> 01:10:44,040
That didn't sound like a real job.

1541
01:10:44,040 --> 01:10:45,180
ChatGPT hadn't come out yet.

1542
01:10:45,180 --> 01:10:47,137
So there's like not even a conception of like,

1543
01:10:47,137 --> 01:10:48,750
"Why would you be doing that?

1544
01:10:48,750 --> 01:10:51,210
That sounds very shameful."

1545
01:10:51,210 --> 01:10:55,170
And so as his wife became more and more confused,

1546
01:10:55,170 --> 01:10:58,770
she also became less and less certain about their marriage.

1547
01:10:58,770 --> 01:11:02,670
And so one day she asks him, "Can we get fish for dinner?"

1548
01:11:02,670 --> 01:11:04,620
He goes out to the store, buys three fish,

1549
01:11:04,620 --> 01:11:07,800
one for him, one for her, one for the stepdaughter.

1550
01:11:07,800 --> 01:11:09,930
And by the time he comes back,

1551
01:11:09,930 --> 01:11:12,180
all of their bags have been packed and they've left.

1552
01:11:12,180 --> 01:11:15,817
And his wife says, texts him and says,

1553
01:11:15,817 --> 01:11:18,570
"I don't know the man you've become anymore.

1554
01:11:18,570 --> 01:11:19,710
We're not coming back."

1555
01:11:19,710 --> 01:11:23,748
- Yeah, I remember Sarah Roberts's book,

1556
01:11:23,748 --> 01:11:27,900
"Behind the Screen," about content moderation,

1557
01:11:27,900 --> 01:11:29,700
commercial content moderation workers.

1558
01:11:29,700 --> 01:11:32,550
So many thousands and thousands of stories

1559
01:11:32,550 --> 01:11:37,140
of people whose lives, whose physical bodies

1560
01:11:37,140 --> 01:11:38,970
had completely deteriorated

1561
01:11:38,970 --> 01:11:41,880
from the weight of this kind of work.

1562
01:11:41,880 --> 01:11:44,850
And this is part of the hidden dimension

1563
01:11:44,850 --> 01:11:49,080
of what it means to also participate in these systems

1564
01:11:49,080 --> 01:11:51,270
and to use these systems certainly.

1565
01:11:51,270 --> 01:11:56,270
And I think of, you know, the water protectors also.

1566
01:11:56,700 --> 01:12:01,470
And, you know, this isn't just in South America

1567
01:12:01,470 --> 01:12:05,610
where people are fighting off large data centers,

1568
01:12:05,610 --> 01:12:08,490
but also Memphis and Austin,

1569
01:12:08,490 --> 01:12:10,800
and places right here in our own,

1570
01:12:10,800 --> 01:12:12,210
and of course we're in California,

1571
01:12:12,210 --> 01:12:14,940
a place that is so environmentally fragile.

1572
01:12:14,940 --> 01:12:16,110
I think it's important

1573
01:12:16,110 --> 01:12:17,520
that we at least kind of name,

1574
01:12:17,520 --> 01:12:18,780
and by we, I mean you, name

1575
01:12:19,804 --> 01:12:21,321
(audience laughs)

1576
01:12:21,321 --> 01:12:24,060
you know, tell us about the environmental cost.

1577
01:12:24,060 --> 01:12:29,060
- Yeah, so in order for Silicon Valley's type of scale,

1578
01:12:29,730 --> 01:12:33,840
at all costs, AI development to perpetuate.

1579
01:12:33,840 --> 01:12:37,020
McKinsey came out with a report recently

1580
01:12:37,020 --> 01:12:40,950
that projected that they have to build massive data centers,

1581
01:12:40,950 --> 01:12:42,240
and massive supercomputers,

1582
01:12:42,240 --> 01:12:44,760
to train the models on all of the data

1583
01:12:44,760 --> 01:12:46,140
that they're harvesting from people.

1584
01:12:46,140 --> 01:12:48,900
And then to actually host the model

1585
01:12:48,900 --> 01:12:51,420
so that when you're chatting with ChatGPT,

1586
01:12:51,420 --> 01:12:54,600
the model has to exist somewhere in like a physical space.

1587
01:12:54,600 --> 01:12:59,010
And the rate at which they are building these data centers

1588
01:12:59,010 --> 01:13:01,980
and supercomputers is historic, it's unprecedented.

1589
01:13:01,980 --> 01:13:05,190
And McKinsey's report said that in the next five years,

1590
01:13:05,190 --> 01:13:07,530
if we keep up the current pace

1591
01:13:07,530 --> 01:13:10,410
and actually build out what these companies are saying

1592
01:13:10,410 --> 01:13:12,510
they will build by 2030,

1593
01:13:12,510 --> 01:13:15,240
we would need two to six times

1594
01:13:15,240 --> 01:13:18,300
the amount of energy consumed in California

1595
01:13:18,300 --> 01:13:20,460
to be added to the global grid.

1596
01:13:20,460 --> 01:13:22,980
And most of that will come from fossil fuels

1597
01:13:22,980 --> 01:13:25,200
because these data centers and supercomputers

1598
01:13:25,200 --> 01:13:27,240
have to run 24/7.

1599
01:13:27,240 --> 01:13:30,690
And Business Insider came out with this investigation

1600
01:13:30,690 --> 01:13:34,447
where they found that utilities across the US are, quote,

1601
01:13:34,447 --> 01:13:38,340
"torpedoing" their renewable energy goals

1602
01:13:38,340 --> 01:13:41,850
in order to sustain the amount of energy

1603
01:13:41,850 --> 01:13:44,640
that needs to be generated for these data centers.

1604
01:13:44,640 --> 01:13:46,530
And I wanna make clear, once again,

1605
01:13:46,530 --> 01:13:48,030
the scale that we're talking about

1606
01:13:48,030 --> 01:13:49,560
is completely different

1607
01:13:49,560 --> 01:13:53,130
than previous internet era companies.

1608
01:13:53,130 --> 01:13:54,990
So during the social media era,

1609
01:13:54,990 --> 01:13:56,820
we also needed data centers,

1610
01:13:56,820 --> 01:13:59,790
but the rate at which data centers were being built

1611
01:13:59,790 --> 01:14:01,770
was roughly equal to the rate

1612
01:14:01,770 --> 01:14:03,990
at which energy efficiency improvements

1613
01:14:03,990 --> 01:14:05,250
were being made on these data centers.

1614
01:14:05,250 --> 01:14:09,300
So in the last decade, the US energy demand has flatlined.

1615
01:14:09,300 --> 01:14:12,510
And in the EU, the energy demand was going down

1616
01:14:12,510 --> 01:14:14,610
even as there were more data centers being built.

1617
01:14:14,610 --> 01:14:16,710
We are now at a point where the data centers

1618
01:14:16,710 --> 01:14:18,960
are being built so quickly

1619
01:14:18,960 --> 01:14:23,340
that we are seeing a historic uptick in energy demand

1620
01:14:23,340 --> 01:14:25,113
around the world and in the US.

1621
01:14:26,154 --> 01:14:29,370
And so we are literally single-handedly reversing

1622
01:14:29,370 --> 01:14:32,250
the climate progress that we made in the last decade

1623
01:14:32,250 --> 01:14:35,070
because of this technology.

1624
01:14:35,070 --> 01:14:38,340
And on top of that, you then have to deal

1625
01:14:38,340 --> 01:14:40,980
with things like public health crises.

1626
01:14:40,980 --> 01:14:42,870
It's exacerbating the air pollution

1627
01:14:42,870 --> 01:14:45,180
that is going primarily into working class communities.

1628
01:14:45,180 --> 01:14:48,630
There's been phenomenal reporting on Grok,

1629
01:14:48,630 --> 01:14:51,510
Elon Musk's company, that is training

1630
01:14:51,510 --> 01:14:53,760
on a supercomputer in Memphis, Tennessee,

1631
01:14:53,760 --> 01:14:56,460
predominantly Black and brown working class communities

1632
01:14:56,460 --> 01:14:59,430
that are now being pummeled by the air pollution

1633
01:14:59,430 --> 01:15:03,990
coming out of 35 unlicensed methane gas turbines

1634
01:15:03,990 --> 01:15:06,300
that are powering the supercomputer.

1635
01:15:06,300 --> 01:15:08,130
And they're now fighting tooth and nail

1636
01:15:08,130 --> 01:15:10,687
because they never even had an opportunity to say,

1637
01:15:10,687 --> 01:15:12,330
"We don't want this supercomputer."

1638
01:15:12,330 --> 01:15:14,190
It just arrived one day.

1639
01:15:14,190 --> 01:15:15,540
And then they started,

1640
01:15:15,540 --> 01:15:17,700
like people who had had chronic asthma

1641
01:15:17,700 --> 01:15:20,010
because this is a community that's already suffered

1642
01:15:20,010 --> 01:15:22,830
in an inability to access

1643
01:15:22,830 --> 01:15:25,650
their right to clean air for a long time,

1644
01:15:25,650 --> 01:15:28,440
they started coughing and smelling gas,

1645
01:15:28,440 --> 01:15:30,000
and being like, "What is going on,"

1646
01:15:30,000 --> 01:15:32,340
and then suddenly discovered this giant

1647
01:15:32,340 --> 01:15:35,790
hulking Silicon apparatus that had landed in their midst.

1648
01:15:35,790 --> 01:15:38,160
And now they're like going to city council,

1649
01:15:38,160 --> 01:15:39,540
fighting with their government,

1650
01:15:39,540 --> 01:15:43,710
being like, "Why did you not even ask us about this?"

1651
01:15:43,710 --> 01:15:45,750
And city government is now over their heads

1652
01:15:45,750 --> 01:15:46,890
because they didn't realize

1653
01:15:46,890 --> 01:15:48,270
that the supercomputer would come

1654
01:15:48,270 --> 01:15:51,180
with these 35 unlicensed gas turbines.

1655
01:15:51,180 --> 01:15:53,910
And they didn't know that Elon Musk would just,

1656
01:15:53,910 --> 01:15:55,680
well, that Elon Musk

1657
01:15:55,680 --> 01:15:57,930
would just buck environmental regulation,

1658
01:15:57,930 --> 01:16:00,660
just totally ignore it to set this up.

1659
01:16:00,660 --> 01:16:03,840
Then there's the public water crisis

1660
01:16:03,840 --> 01:16:05,730
that comes with building these data centers.

1661
01:16:05,730 --> 01:16:08,040
So one thing people don't often know

1662
01:16:08,040 --> 01:16:11,760
is that these data centers are most often pooled with water.

1663
01:16:11,760 --> 01:16:13,680
You can cool them with energy

1664
01:16:13,680 --> 01:16:18,060
by running giant AC units basically in these facilities,

1665
01:16:18,060 --> 01:16:20,370
but that is way more energy intensive.

1666
01:16:20,370 --> 01:16:22,560
And so the best way to do it is cool it with water.

1667
01:16:22,560 --> 01:16:27,360
But if it's cooled with water, it has to be freshwater.

1668
01:16:27,360 --> 01:16:29,700
It cannot be any other type of water

1669
01:16:29,700 --> 01:16:32,820
because that would lead to the corrosion of the equipment

1670
01:16:32,820 --> 01:16:34,380
or bacterial growth.

1671
01:16:34,380 --> 01:16:37,350
And so Bloomberg had an investigation

1672
01:16:37,350 --> 01:16:40,620
that found that two-thirds of the data centers being built

1673
01:16:40,620 --> 01:16:45,060
to support the AI industry now are going into communities

1674
01:16:45,060 --> 01:16:47,640
that do not have enough freshwater

1675
01:16:47,640 --> 01:16:50,550
for the human population there.

1676
01:16:50,550 --> 01:16:54,270
That includes California, that includes Arizona,

1677
01:16:54,270 --> 01:16:57,480
where this southwestern United States

1678
01:16:57,480 --> 01:16:59,760
has faced a historic drought,

1679
01:16:59,760 --> 01:17:02,370
the worst drought in a thousand years,

1680
01:17:02,370 --> 01:17:04,110
while it has also become

1681
01:17:04,110 --> 01:17:06,630
the second largest data center hub in the US,

1682
01:17:06,630 --> 01:17:08,550
potentially the world.

1683
01:17:08,550 --> 01:17:11,400
And if you go further afield,

1684
01:17:11,400 --> 01:17:14,970
I went to Uruguay to meet with a community there

1685
01:17:14,970 --> 01:17:17,220
that was also fighting data center development there.

1686
01:17:17,220 --> 01:17:19,080
And they were facing historic drought

1687
01:17:19,080 --> 01:17:22,260
that was so bad that the Montevideo government

1688
01:17:22,260 --> 01:17:23,850
started mixing saltwater

1689
01:17:23,850 --> 01:17:25,710
into the public drinking water supply

1690
01:17:25,710 --> 01:17:30,060
because there was not enough water to service people's taps.

1691
01:17:30,060 --> 01:17:33,510
And for people who could not pay for bottled water,

1692
01:17:33,510 --> 01:17:35,040
that is the water they were drinking.

1693
01:17:35,040 --> 01:17:37,560
And women were having higher rates of miscarriages,

1694
01:17:37,560 --> 01:17:40,830
people with chronic illnesses were having inflamed symptoms.

1695
01:17:40,830 --> 01:17:42,780
And it was in the middle of that,

1696
01:17:42,780 --> 01:17:44,610
when these people were already fighting

1697
01:17:44,610 --> 01:17:47,010
for access to life-sustaining resources

1698
01:17:47,010 --> 01:17:48,037
that Google was like,

1699
01:17:48,037 --> 01:17:49,980
"We want your life-sustaining resources

1700
01:17:49,980 --> 01:17:51,447
to cool our data center."

1701
01:17:52,500 --> 01:17:56,010
- Karen, I think about your work and your voice

1702
01:17:56,010 --> 01:18:00,450
as a light in the complete darkness.

1703
01:18:00,450 --> 01:18:05,450
Like, we're sitting here in the heart of Silicon Valley

1704
01:18:05,760 --> 01:18:09,960
and the weight,

1705
01:18:09,960 --> 01:18:12,030
I can just feel the weight

1706
01:18:12,030 --> 01:18:15,390
of the AI hype cycle that is around us.

1707
01:18:15,390 --> 01:18:18,360
It was on the escalator, if you saw that coming up.

1708
01:18:18,360 --> 01:18:21,675
There's like some escalators taking you to AI heaven.

1709
01:18:21,675 --> 01:18:22,911
I don't know what the onset-

1710
01:18:22,911 --> 01:18:24,013
(audience laughing)

1711
01:18:24,013 --> 01:18:27,420
It felt like it said something like that.

1712
01:18:27,420 --> 01:18:29,130
And yet, you know, I know we are talking

1713
01:18:29,130 --> 01:18:32,370
about large language models for the most part.

1714
01:18:32,370 --> 01:18:35,160
Our friend and colleague, Dr. Timnit Gebru,

1715
01:18:35,160 --> 01:18:38,040
and her incredible organization DAIR,

1716
01:18:38,040 --> 01:18:39,960
has been, and I know you've been looking

1717
01:18:39,960 --> 01:18:44,960
at other kinds of AI and, you know, you close the book

1718
01:18:45,720 --> 01:18:46,920
on a hopeful note,

1719
01:18:46,920 --> 01:18:49,800
and I'm gonna attempt to close this session

1720
01:18:49,800 --> 01:18:50,763
on a hopeful note.

1721
01:18:51,630 --> 01:18:53,985
Not guaranteeing that that will be the outcome.

1722
01:18:53,985 --> 01:18:55,350
(audience laughing)

1723
01:18:55,350 --> 01:18:57,539
But you've got all day to recover.

1724
01:18:57,539 --> 01:18:59,302
(audience continues laughing)

1725
01:18:59,302 --> 01:19:00,135
(audience member applauds)

1726
01:19:00,135 --> 01:19:04,203
You know, this idea of kind of like small task-based AI.

1727
01:19:05,340 --> 01:19:07,980
How could we think about AI,

1728
01:19:07,980 --> 01:19:11,160
AI, you know, is a big catchall for a lot of things,

1729
01:19:11,160 --> 01:19:15,030
but is there some promise,

1730
01:19:15,030 --> 01:19:17,040
and how do we disambiguate it

1731
01:19:17,040 --> 01:19:18,570
from the things you've talked about

1732
01:19:18,570 --> 01:19:20,250
that we know we don't want?

1733
01:19:20,250 --> 01:19:21,690
- Yeah, so I mentioned earlier

1734
01:19:21,690 --> 01:19:23,670
that there are a full range

1735
01:19:23,670 --> 01:19:25,290
of different types of AI technologies.

1736
01:19:25,290 --> 01:19:26,520
And I think really the best way

1737
01:19:26,520 --> 01:19:29,130
to think about the word AI is like the word transportation.

1738
01:19:29,130 --> 01:19:31,890
There's a whole lot of different transportation options.

1739
01:19:31,890 --> 01:19:33,360
You can have bicycles, rockets,

1740
01:19:33,360 --> 01:19:35,850
self-driving cars, gas-guzzling trucks.

1741
01:19:35,850 --> 01:19:37,380
And generally speaking,

1742
01:19:37,380 --> 01:19:40,260
we don't say we just need more transportation

1743
01:19:40,260 --> 01:19:41,160
to solve our problems, right?

1744
01:19:41,160 --> 01:19:45,270
We understand that we need to electrify our transportation,

1745
01:19:45,270 --> 01:19:48,903
we need to ride more bikes than fly more rockets.

1746
01:19:51,060 --> 01:19:53,580
Like, I really do think that the AI models

1747
01:19:53,580 --> 01:19:54,780
that are being produced by Silicon Valley

1748
01:19:54,780 --> 01:19:58,230
are kind of like the rocket equivalent in transportation

1749
01:19:58,230 --> 01:19:59,790
where, generally speaking,

1750
01:19:59,790 --> 01:20:02,790
there's actually a very narrow range of transportation needs

1751
01:20:02,790 --> 01:20:05,070
where we actually need the rocket.

1752
01:20:05,070 --> 01:20:07,470
And it would make utterly no sense

1753
01:20:07,470 --> 01:20:10,440
for us to give everyone a rocket to use for everything.

1754
01:20:10,440 --> 01:20:12,210
And suddenly you're commuting from LA

1755
01:20:12,210 --> 01:20:14,295
to Santa Clara on a rocket.

1756
01:20:14,295 --> 01:20:15,330
(audience laughing)

1757
01:20:15,330 --> 01:20:17,250
Yeah, like that's just not fit for purpose.

1758
01:20:17,250 --> 01:20:21,810
And I hope that we can move the conversation in AI

1759
01:20:21,810 --> 01:20:25,170
to start disentangling the nuances there as well.

1760
01:20:25,170 --> 01:20:27,420
But the thing that I write about

1761
01:20:27,420 --> 01:20:28,770
in my epilogue for the book

1762
01:20:28,770 --> 01:20:32,760
is about this story of this community in New Zealand

1763
01:20:32,760 --> 01:20:34,140
called Te Hiku Media.

1764
01:20:34,140 --> 01:20:37,290
It's one of the best stories that I've ever reported.

1765
01:20:37,290 --> 01:20:40,440
They're a Maori radio station

1766
01:20:40,440 --> 01:20:42,990
that really wanted to contribute to the revitalization

1767
01:20:42,990 --> 01:20:44,580
of the Te Reo Maori language

1768
01:20:44,580 --> 01:20:47,310
because, like many Indigenous peoples around the world,

1769
01:20:47,310 --> 01:20:49,650
Maori have almost lost their language

1770
01:20:49,650 --> 01:20:53,070
from racist assimilation policies during colonization.

1771
01:20:53,070 --> 01:20:55,650
And Te Hiku Media has been broadcasting

1772
01:20:55,650 --> 01:20:58,350
in the Te Reo Maori language for decades,

1773
01:20:58,350 --> 01:21:00,510
as one of the only organizations

1774
01:21:00,510 --> 01:21:03,210
that has that really long continuous history.

1775
01:21:03,210 --> 01:21:07,050
And so they thought we wanna bring this archival audio

1776
01:21:07,050 --> 01:21:08,490
to Te Reo Maori learners

1777
01:21:08,490 --> 01:21:11,040
so that they can hear the sounds of their ancestors

1778
01:21:11,040 --> 01:21:14,220
and actually reclaim their own language.

1779
01:21:14,220 --> 01:21:16,890
And so the problem was in order to do that

1780
01:21:16,890 --> 01:21:19,950
they wanted to create this interactive education tool

1781
01:21:19,950 --> 01:21:21,840
where they could transcribe the audio

1782
01:21:21,840 --> 01:21:23,490
so people could listen to the audio

1783
01:21:23,490 --> 01:21:27,510
while they saw the words coming up on the screen,

1784
01:21:27,510 --> 01:21:28,860
and then they could click on the words

1785
01:21:28,860 --> 01:21:31,140
and see the meaning, the translation,

1786
01:21:31,140 --> 01:21:33,030
see how it's used in other contexts

1787
01:21:33,030 --> 01:21:35,730
so that they can really learn this language.

1788
01:21:35,730 --> 01:21:38,700
But there weren't enough Te Reo Maori speakers

1789
01:21:38,700 --> 01:21:43,257
with sufficient fluency to actually do that transcription.

1790
01:21:43,257 --> 01:21:46,080
And so they thought, "We wanna use AI."

1791
01:21:46,080 --> 01:21:48,750
But they went about developing their AI tool

1792
01:21:48,750 --> 01:21:51,180
in a fundamentally different way.

1793
01:21:51,180 --> 01:21:54,307
First and foremost, they went to their community and asked,

1794
01:21:54,307 --> 01:21:56,670
"Do you want AI?"

1795
01:21:56,670 --> 01:21:58,500
The community said yes.

1796
01:21:58,500 --> 01:22:01,410
And then, with their consent,

1797
01:22:01,410 --> 01:22:03,547
they then taught the community,

1798
01:22:03,547 --> 01:22:05,250
"This is how AI usually works.

1799
01:22:05,250 --> 01:22:06,810
We're going to need your data.

1800
01:22:06,810 --> 01:22:09,660
We're going to need donations from you

1801
01:22:09,660 --> 01:22:12,630
of audio with transcription

1802
01:22:12,630 --> 01:22:15,720
so that we can train the speech recognition tool

1803
01:22:15,720 --> 01:22:18,600
that knows how to transcribe Te Reo Maori."

1804
01:22:18,600 --> 01:22:20,700
And the community was really excited

1805
01:22:20,700 --> 01:22:24,030
because they knew that it could actually benefit them

1806
01:22:24,030 --> 01:22:26,790
and not exacerbate historical inequities,

1807
01:22:26,790 --> 01:22:30,540
but actually reverse and give them back power.

1808
01:22:30,540 --> 01:22:34,200
And so they were more than happy to give their data.

1809
01:22:34,200 --> 01:22:37,860
And because there was so much buy-in and so much trust,

1810
01:22:37,860 --> 01:22:42,150
within like three days, they were able to gather enough data

1811
01:22:42,150 --> 01:22:43,530
to train a model.

1812
01:22:43,530 --> 01:22:48,180
And because this was a small task-specific AI system,

1813
01:22:48,180 --> 01:22:49,380
not an everything machine,

1814
01:22:49,380 --> 01:22:52,470
it was just doing one task really, really well,

1815
01:22:52,470 --> 01:22:54,810
they got away with just training

1816
01:22:54,810 --> 01:22:58,140
on a couple hundred hours of audio

1817
01:22:58,140 --> 01:23:01,020
and they were able to get to a high enough proficiency

1818
01:23:01,020 --> 01:23:04,650
that they could start transcribing their archive.

1819
01:23:04,650 --> 01:23:06,637
And then they went back to the community and said,

1820
01:23:06,637 --> 01:23:08,250
"Now that we have this tool,

1821
01:23:08,250 --> 01:23:09,780
how do you want us to use it?

1822
01:23:09,780 --> 01:23:12,450
What kind of educational tools do you want?

1823
01:23:12,450 --> 01:23:16,320
And what other AI tools do you want us to build with it?"

1824
01:23:16,320 --> 01:23:19,717
And another part was they promised the community,

1825
01:23:19,717 --> 01:23:21,810
"Now that we have your data,

1826
01:23:21,810 --> 01:23:24,450
we're going to be guardians of that data.

1827
01:23:24,450 --> 01:23:26,262
We're not gonna sell that data,

1828
01:23:26,262 --> 01:23:28,975
we're not gonna try to monetize that data.

1829
01:23:28,975 --> 01:23:31,440
We are going to create a legally binding license

1830
01:23:31,440 --> 01:23:34,410
so that if anyone ever wants access to this data,

1831
01:23:34,410 --> 01:23:38,070
they have to sign off on our community's values

1832
01:23:38,070 --> 01:23:40,260
to make sure that this data is never used

1833
01:23:40,260 --> 01:23:44,400
in a project that might ultimately come back to harm us."

